{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:historian","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:21590f81a884fb430e314ee977da938875ea8038555d0e1a2cd73f30fd3efe9a","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.18},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Conjunct A (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core inflation >= 2.50%/yr average 2027-2036): REFERENCE CLASS 1 = all OECD 10-year windows since 1960: roughly 1960-1999 windows all clear 2.5% (1970s ~8-9%, 1980s ~5-6%, 1990s ~3%) while 2000-2019 windows do not (2000s ~1.9-2.1%, 2010s ~1.4-1.6%), giving ~60-65% unconditional. REFERENCE CLASS 2 (narrower, structurally honest) = credible inflation-targeting-era windows, 1995-2026: almost all print 1.6-2.3%, with only the most recent windows pulled to ~2.3-2.6% by the 2021-23 shock, giving ~20-25%. I weighted the IT-era class ~60/40 because the regime break (independent central banks, explicit 2% targets) is mechanical rather than mechanism-specific, landing at 0.35. Conjunct B (China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr average 2027-2036): REFERENCE CLASS = China CPI 10-year windows since 1980: decade averages ~7% (1980s), ~7.7% (1990s), ~1.9% (2000s), ~2.3% (2010-19), ~0.6% (2020-26); only a handful of ~37 rolling windows (late-1990s deflation windows, 2015-24, 2017-26) land below 1.50%, ~15-20% unconditional. But the governing class is persistence out of a post-property-bust demographic-contraction trap, where the reference set is essentially Japan 1995-2020: CPI averaged ~0.1%/yr for 25 years and the base rate of exiting within a decade is 0 of 2 decades. Conditional on the locked 2A state (CPI ~0-0.5%, -60M population 2026-35, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8), B = 0.58. CORRELATION: the conjunction requires a SUSTAINED OECD-vs-China inflation DIVERGENCE, and OECD/EM inflation co-moves positively through commodities and traded goods, which argues for a discount; offsetting that, the OECD-vs-Japan analogue shows exactly this ~2pp divergence held for 25 years, and it held in precisely the demographic-contraction-plus-balance-sheet case. I therefore used a correlation near zero-to-slightly-negative, P(B|A) = 0.52. JOINT = 0.35 x 0.52 = 0.182 -> 18. Note the two-sided 2A content I did NOT resolve by mechanism: Goodhart-Pradhan aging-as-inflationary (labour scarcity, dissaving) versus BIS AER 2026 Box C aging/automation-as-demand-shortfall. The historical record contains both signs, which is itself a reason not to sharpen.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.133Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD Earnings/Health at a Glance data show the OECD-average real hourly wage of personal-care and LTC workers rising >= 12% cumulatively ABOVE the whole-economy average between 2025 and 2030, reversing the Beyond Applause below-average finding.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031 (publication of the 2030 reference year)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my central claim that administered care wages cannot clear without a fiscal transfer 2A says is strained. CQ1's wage-divergence conjunct moves from 0.20 to ~0.40 and CQ1 from 12 to ~25-28; hypothesis plank (a) moves from 5 to ~20; CQ6-C gains ~3pp from B on the Baumol-cost channel.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"In any OECD country-year, the share of new RN or LPN licensees aged 40+ exceeds 15% (versus a historical ~6-8%), or a national statistical agency publishes a flow showing >= 10% of workers exiting ISIC J/K/M entering ISIC Q within three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the stock-flow mismatch and the ~0.05-0.10 cross-elasticity that drive my low CQ1 and my 5 on plank (a). Plank (a) moves to ~25-30, CQ1's s